findings.body:
`FormWrapper` now reads `openForm.in_reply_to_snippet` with no optional chaining. The original line was `openForm?.in_reply_to_snippet`, and the PR removed the `?.`.

::diff{file="apps/comments-ui/src/components/content/forms/form.tsx" lines="310-316"}

`MainForm` (`main-form.tsx` line 98) renders `<FormWrapper editor={editor} isOpen={isOpen} reduced={false}>` with no `openForm`. Its `isOpen` is `editor?.isFocused || hasContent`, so it becomes true as soon as someone clicks into the top-level box or types. At that point `isOpen` is true, `openForm` is `undefined`, and the property read throws `TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined`. That takes down the whole comments app. The reply-form path is unaffected, which is why the new e2e tests don't catch it.

::callout{type="warning"}
`openForm` is an optional prop (`openForm?: OpenCommentForm`) and the project compiles with `strict: true`, so `tsc` should flag this. Either the typecheck isn't run on this change or it is failing. Check both.

Restore the optional chaining.

::suggestion{file="apps/comments-ui/src/components/content/forms/form.tsx" lines="313-313"}
        const isReplyToReply = !!openForm?.in_reply_to_snippet;

The editable gate changed from `!!memberName` to `!!member?.expertise`:

::diff{file="apps/comments-ui/src/components/content/forms/form.tsx" lines="261-265"}

The add-details popup only requires a name. Expertise is optional and can be saved empty (`add-details-popup.tsx` lines 38-50 check only `name.trim()`). So a member who has entered a name but no expertise is a valid state, and this change makes the editor read-only for them in both the reply form and the main form.

The effect also depends on `member`, so after the user saves their name in the popup, the `callback` calls `editor.setEditable(true)`. The effect then re-runs on the member change and immediately sets it back to `false`. That leaves a name-only member stuck with a read-only editor after completing the details flow.

The PR description only talks about the header, so this gating change looks unintended. Restore the name-based gate, or, if expertise really should gate posting, state that in the PR and add a test.

::suggestion{file="apps/comments-ui/src/components/content/forms/form.tsx" lines="264-265"}
        editor.setEditable(!!member?.name && progress !== 'sending');
    }, [editor, member?.name, progress]);

The core fix is right. `FormWrapper` and `Form` previously read `comment?.member?.name` and `comment?.member?.expertise` as fallbacks, so a logged-in member with no name or expertise was shown the parent comment author's details in the reply header. Reading only `member?.name` and `member?.expertise` fixes that. The `comment` prop is also removed from `FormWrapper` and from its only caller in `reply-form.tsx`, and `main-form.tsx` never passed it, so no call site is left dangling.

::diff{file="apps/comments-ui/src/components/content/forms/form.tsx" lines="305-312"}

The two new e2e tests reproduce the reported scenario directly (logged-in member with null name or expertise, a parent with both set), so they would have failed before this change.
